# The vibrator this device actually has.
#
# 🔴 Found 2026-08-19, once the vibrator HAL started running for the first time
# (its -stock rename had no exec type until today, so it never ran to be denied).
#
# There are TWO vibrator nodes here and MediaTek labels only the simple one:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/genfs_contexts:268
#     /devices/platform/soc/soc:regulator_vibrator/leds/vibrator -> sysfs_vibrator
# Stock's HAL drives the other one -- an I2C AWINIC-class LRA at 0-0058, with
# f0/cali/lra_resistance/osc_cali/ram_update/rtp/gain, i.e. real haptics rather
# than a on/off motor -- and nothing labelled it, so it landed as plain sysfs:
#   avc: denied { read write } for comm="android.hardwar" name="activate"
#     scontext=u:r:hal_vibrator_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0
#
# Only the LABEL is missing. MediaTek already grants the permission:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/hal_vibrator_default.te
#     allow hal_vibrator_default sysfs_vibrator:file rw_file_perms;
# Confirmed live with chcon on the running device before this was written: the
# denials stopped and did not come back.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/11e00000.i2c/i2c-0/0-0058/leds/vibrator_single u:object_r:sysfs_vibrator:s0

# /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs -- the third of three sibling knobs,
# and the one AOSP forgot to label:
#   system/sepolicy/private/genfs_contexts:82  /sys/vm/dirty_background_ratio  -> proc_dirty
#   system/sepolicy/private/genfs_contexts:83  /sys/vm/dirty_expire_centisecs  -> proc_dirty
#   (no entry at all for dirty_writeback_centisecs)
#
# Unlabelled it resolves to plain `proc`, which vendor_init may open, read,
# setattr and map but NOT write (system/sepolicy/public/vendor_init.te:143).
# So stock's own tuning line, which this tree extracts verbatim --
#   rootdir/etc/init/hw/init.mt6789.rc:821  write /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs 300
#   (stock's init.mt6789.rc:800, byte for byte)
# -- fails silently. Measured on build 71: one avc denial at boot, and
#   /proc/sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs = 500
# which is the kernel default, not the 300 stock asks for. init reports no error
# for a failed `write`, so nothing surfaces it.
#
# Labelling it proc_dirty is what its two siblings already carry, and no
# neverallow names proc_dirty or restricts writing proc_type. Labelling a
# /proc/sys/vm node from vendor policy follows MediaTek's own precedent:
#   sepolicy_vndr/basic/non_plat/genfs_contexts:125
#     genfscon proc /sys/vm/swappiness u:object_r:proc_swappiness:s0
genfscon proc /sys/vm/dirty_writeback_centisecs u:object_r:proc_dirty:s0

# /proc/sys/kernel/sched_pelt_multiplier is an MTK addition, so AOSP's
# genfs_contexts does not know it and it falls to the generic proc:s0 -- which
# init cannot write. Every sysctl init writes has a dedicated type
# (proc_sched, proc_watermark_scale_factor, proc_net ...); this gives ours the
# one AOSP already grants:
#   public/file.te:64   type proc_sched, fs_type, proc_type;
#   public/init.te:396  allow init { ... proc_sched ... }:file w_file_perms;
# Without this the init.cgroup.rc on property:sys.gamemode.active writes are
# denied and the game-scoped PELT ramp ships DEAD -- silently, because it fails
# as a refused write and not as anything a boot sweep reads.
genfscon proc /sys/kernel/sched_pelt_multiplier u:object_r:proc_sched:s0

# The four power_supply nodes nobody labels -- and the one that made Android
# believe the phone was never plugged in.
#
# 🔴 Found 2026-08-31 from a user bug report ("it doesn't charge"), and the
# phone WAS charging the whole time: the framework simply could not see the
# charger. On the 45 W charger, before this:
#     dumpsys battery -> AC powered: false   USB powered: false
#                        Max charging current: 0   Max charging voltage: 0
#                        status: 2 (Charging)
# So the battery icon carries no bolt, Settings offers a DISCHARGE estimate,
# and every third-party battery app reads "not plugged in" while the battery
# is taking 4 A.
#
# Mechanism. system/core/healthd/BatteryMonitor.cpp:376-390 scans
# /sys/class/power_supply ONCE in init() and enrols a supply as a charger only
# if it can READ that supply's `type` file. AOSP's public/hal_health.te grants
#     allow hal_health_server sysfs:dir r_dir_perms;   <- LIST the class dir
#     r_dir_file(hal_health_server, sysfs_batteryinfo) <- READ the files
# and says so in as many words: "Vendors should mark sysfs_batteryinfo on all
# files read by health HAL service." The only supply on this device whose type
# is ever USB/USB_DCP/Mains is `charger` -- the Southchip main charger at
# i2c-6 0x6b -- and neither MediaTek's sepolicy_vndr (which labels 19 other
# charger paths, basic/non_plat/genfs_contexts:173-191) nor stock's own
# vendor_sepolicy.cil has an entry for it. It therefore falls to plain sysfs,
# readPowerSupplyType() fails, and the supply is invisible for the life of the
# process. mtk-master-charger IS labelled by sepolicy_vndr:173 but its type is
# "Unknown", so it can never stand in.
#
# 🔴 THIS DEFECT EMITS NO DENIAL, FOR TWO INDEPENDENT REASONS, AND EITHER ONE
# ALONE WOULD HAVE HIDDEN IT.
#   1. readPowerSupplyType() returns UNKNOWN on ANY read failure and logs
#      nothing -- a refused read is indistinguishable from "Unknown" here.
#   2. sepolicy/vendor/dontaudit.te silenced the avc record itself on
#      2026-08-11, reasoning "the battery UI is correct on the running build".
#      It was not; nobody had looked at the PLUG state. That dontaudit is
#      removed in the same change as this comment.
#
# ✅ VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-08-31, both charger classes, by chcon on the
# running device and a restart of health-hal-2-1 -- reverted and re-applied so
# the A/B is a real A/B:
#     PC USB (charger/type=USB)        -> USB powered: true
#     45 W charger (type=USB_DCP)      -> AC  powered: true
#                                         Max charging current 3,689,400 uA
#                                         Max charging voltage 4,227,000 uV
#
# The other three entries are not needed for that fix; they are here so that
# every entry under /sys/class/power_supply is readable and REMOVING the
# dontaudit leaves a clean sweep. Neither of the two that carry an AC-ish type
# changes what Android reports:
#   primary_dvchg  type=Mains but has NO `online` file, and BatteryMonitor
#                  enrols a supply only if access(online, R_OK) == 0.
#   usb_switch     type=Mains, online=1 on the wall charger and 0 on a PC
#                  port -- i.e. it agrees with `charger`. It has no
#                  current_max/voltage_max, and this branch of BatteryMonitor
#                  takes the max-power reading from the BATTERY's own
#                  current_now/voltage_now anyway (:440-450), so it cannot
#                  skew the reported charging rate either.
#   mtk_charger_type  type=Unknown; never enrolled.
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/1101a000.i2c/i2c-6/6-006b/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/11eb3000.i2c/i2c-9/9-006a/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/odm/odm:usb_switch/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
genfscon sysfs /devices/platform/soc/10026000.pwrap/10026000.pwrap:mt6366/mt6358-chrdet/power_supply u:object_r:sysfs_batteryinfo:s0
